### What is this question really testing?

The interviewer is screening for self-awareness and coachability. They want to eliminate two bad patterns: the candidate who claims they never received negative feedback (unbelievable), and the candidate who got defensive or blamed the giver.

```java
package com.bank.interview;
public class FeedbackTest {
    enum Signal { COACHABLE, DEFENSIVE, UNAWARE }
    public static Signal evaluate(boolean accepted, boolean changed, boolean verified) {
        if (!accepted) return Signal.DEFENSIVE;
        if (!changed) return Signal.UNAWARE;
        return Signal.COACHABLE;
    }
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(evaluate(true, true, true)); // COACHABLE
    }
}
```

### What was the specific feedback?

At National Bank of India, a senior engineer told me that my pull requests were too large. I was bundling account creation, transfer logic, and reporting into one giant change, making reviews slow and risky.

```java
package com.bank.interview;
public class TheFeedback {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Feedback: 'Your pull requests are too large.'");
        System.out.println("Evidence: account creation + transfer + reporting in one PR");
        System.out.println("Impact: reviews took days, reviewers could not hold it all in their head");
    }
}
```

### How did you manage the emotional reaction?

I admitted it stung briefly, then recognized the feedback was completely valid. Managing the reaction is as important as the change.

```java
package com.bank.interview;
public class EmotionalManagement {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Honest: 'It stung at first because I thought I was being efficient.'");
        System.out.println("Then: 'But I recognized it was valid and thanked him.'");
        System.out.println("Key: admit the sting briefly, then focus on the signal.");
    }
}
```

### How did you make a concrete, measurable change?

I started keeping every pull request under a clear size budget: roughly ten files and a few hundred lines. I split large features into small, independently reviewable changes.

```java
package com.bank.service;
public class PRSizeBudget {
    private static final int MAX_FILES = 10;
    private static final int MAX_LINES = 400;
    public static boolean isReviewable(int files, int lines) {
        return files <= MAX_FILES && lines <= MAX_LINES;
    }
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("PR with 8 files, 300 lines: " + isReviewable(8, 300));  // true
        System.out.println("PR with 25 files, 1200 lines: " + isReviewable(25, 1200)); // false
    }
}
```

```text
PR with 8 files, 300 lines: true
PR with 25 files, 1200 lines: false
```

### How did you close the loop?

I went back to the same engineer a few weeks later and asked whether my pull requests were easier to review. He confirmed they were, and review turnaround dropped dramatically.

```java
package com.bank.interview;
public class ClosingTheLoop {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Week 0: Received feedback about PR size");
        System.out.println("Week 1-3: Applied size budget to all PRs");
        System.out.println("Week 4: Asked the same engineer for follow-up");
        System.out.println("Result: 'Much better, reviews take hours not days'");
    }
}
```

### How did you turn it into a systemic habit?

Instead of relying on willpower, I made the PR size limit an objective check. If a change exceeds the budget, I split it before submitting.

```java
package com.bank.service;
public class SystemicHabit {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Before: relied on memory to keep PRs small -> inconsistent");
        System.out.println("After: objective size budget (10 files, 400 lines) -> consistent");
        System.out.println("The system enforces the behavior, not willpower.");
    }
}
```

### What if the feedback was wrong?

Sometimes feedback is genuinely off-base. Even then, the professional response is to listen, evaluate honestly, and explain your reasoning calmly if you disagree. The point is the process, not blind obedience.

```java
package com.bank.interview;
public class WrongFeedback {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("If feedback is valid: accept and change");
        System.out.println("If feedback is wrong: listen, evaluate, explain calmly");
        System.out.println("Never: get defensive, blame the giver, or dismiss without thought");
    }
}
```

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

Expect: "What if you disagree with the feedback?" (listen, evaluate, explain), "Who gave it?" (be specific but professional about the person), and "Has it come up again?" (no, because the system prevents it).

```java
package com.bank.interview;
public class FeedbackFollowUps {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Disagree? -> Listen, evaluate honestly, explain if warranted");
        System.out.println("Who? -> A senior engineer I respected; his perspective was valuable");
        System.out.println("Come up again? -> No, the size budget prevents it");
    }
}
```
